WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.142
Here's a bet nobody sane would take.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:07.593
You spend two hundred million dollars and ten years

00:00:07.593 --> 00:00:09.251
on one cancer drug.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.644
One time in twenty it works,

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:15.220
and then it's worth about twelve billion dollars.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:18.212
The other nineteen times you get nothing.

00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:21.714
Andrew Lo at MIT asked a simple question.

00:00:21.714 --> 00:00:24.712
What if you made a hundred and fifty of

00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:26.173
these bets at once?

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.893
Think of each drug as a weighted coin that

00:00:29.893 --> 00:00:32.882
lands heads five percent of the time.

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.871
Now flip a hundred and fifty of them.

00:00:36.221 --> 00:00:39.521
On average you get seven and a half winners.

00:00:39.521 --> 00:00:42.897
Here's how often you get each possible count,

00:00:42.897 --> 00:00:45.672
if the coins don't affect each other.

00:00:46.022 --> 00:00:50.097
Lo's bonds default if fewer than two drugs work.

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:53.833
That happens about four times in a thousand.

00:00:54.183 --> 00:00:57.722
Something that safe can be sold as a bond,

00:00:57.722 --> 00:01:01.429
and pension funds buy bonds by the trillion.

00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:03.114
That's the megafund.

00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:06.638
The catch is the word independent.

00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:09.654
Drugs share biology, regulators,

00:01:09.654 --> 00:01:12.764
and fashions in what gets funded.

00:01:13.114 --> 00:01:15.798
So give every coin a shared nudge.

00:01:15.798 --> 00:01:17.534
When the nudge is bad,

00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:21.323
every coin gets a little worse at the same time.

00:01:21.323 --> 00:01:24.086
I set its correlation to point two,

00:01:24.086 --> 00:01:27.085
the kind of model Lo's own group uses.

00:01:27.435 --> 00:01:29.275
The average doesn't move.

00:01:29.275 --> 00:01:31.336
It's still seven and a half.

00:01:31.336 --> 00:01:34.206
But look at the left side of the chart.

00:01:34.556 --> 00:01:39.148
Now the bonds default about one time in five.

00:01:39.148 --> 00:01:43.127
One run in ten produces no drug at all.

00:01:43.477 --> 00:01:46.284
There's a neat rule behind this.

00:01:46.284 --> 00:01:48.126
With correlation rho,

00:01:48.126 --> 00:01:51.283
N bets behave like N over one plus N

00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:54.528
minus one times rho independent ones.

00:01:54.878 --> 00:01:57.748
Make N as big as you like and you

00:01:57.748 --> 00:02:00.183
never get past one over rho.

00:02:00.183 --> 00:02:02.793
At a correlation of point one,

00:02:02.793 --> 00:02:05.663
that's ten bets' worth of safety,

00:02:05.663 --> 00:02:07.489
however many you own.

00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:10.228
And these numbers are real now.

00:02:10.228 --> 00:02:10.999
This June,

00:02:10.999 --> 00:02:17.088
Lo's group estimated correlations across more than twenty-five thousand trials.

00:02:17.088 --> 00:02:20.325
About point one inside a therapeutic area.

00:02:20.325 --> 00:02:24.410
Up to point four among trials that share a mechanism.

00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:30.333
BridgeBio is the closest anyone has come to building this.

00:02:30.333 --> 00:02:32.357
More than thirty programs,

00:02:32.357 --> 00:02:35.628
mostly in unrelated rare genetic diseases.

00:02:35.978 --> 00:02:41.590
In December 2021 one heart drug missed its twelve-month endpoint.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.389
The stock fell sixty-seven percent that day.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:49.339
Over the next months the company cut about thirty

00:02:49.339 --> 00:02:52.939
percent of its staff and paused programs that had

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:55.070
nothing to do with that drug.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.782
What tied them together was the stock price and

00:02:58.782 --> 00:03:01.501
more than two billion dollars of debt.

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:04.076
The drugs were close to independent.

00:03:04.076 --> 00:03:07.510
The money behind them was one big shared factor.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.130
The heart drug won at thirty months anyway,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.562
and the FDA approved it in 2024.

00:03:13.562 --> 00:03:16.831
A trial database would never see that link.

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:20.650
There's another way to make money from drug risk,

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.233
and it's the opposite of a megafund.

00:03:23.233 --> 00:03:26.103
You don't own a hundred and fifty coins.

00:03:26.103 --> 00:03:28.902
You try to learn how one coin will land

00:03:28.902 --> 00:03:30.624
before anyone else does.

00:03:30.974 --> 00:03:35.700
Bapineuzumab was an Alzheimer's antibody from Elan and Wyeth.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:37.482
In 2008 Mathew Martoma,

00:03:37.482 --> 00:03:41.046
a portfolio manager in the SAC Capital family,

00:03:41.046 --> 00:03:45.074
was paying a neurologist named Sidney Gilman about a

00:03:45.074 --> 00:03:49.025
thousand dollars an hour through an expert network.

00:03:49.375 --> 00:03:52.304
Gilman chaired the trial's safety committee.

00:03:52.304 --> 00:03:55.765
About two weeks before the detailed results came out

00:03:55.765 --> 00:03:57.629
on the twenty-ninth of July,

00:03:57.629 --> 00:03:59.626
he told Martoma they were bad.

00:03:59.976 --> 00:04:03.629
The funds held more than seven hundred million dollars

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:04.847
of Elan and Wyeth.

00:04:04.847 --> 00:04:07.485
In just over a week they sold more than

00:04:07.485 --> 00:04:11.747
nine hundred and sixty million dollars of stock and went short.

00:04:12.097 --> 00:04:16.677
The SEC counted eighty-two million dollars of profit and

00:04:16.677 --> 00:04:20.930
a hundred and ninety-four million of losses avoided.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:26.247
That's two hundred and seventy-six million dollars from one coin,

00:04:26.247 --> 00:04:27.228
known early.

00:04:27.578 --> 00:04:29.963
Martoma got nine years in prison,

00:04:29.963 --> 00:04:32.998
and bapineuzumab failed in Phase 3 anyway.

00:04:32.998 --> 00:04:36.828
Leave the crime aside and the arithmetic still holds.

00:04:36.828 --> 00:04:40.441
Knowing one outcome better than the price is where

00:04:40.441 --> 00:04:41.381
the money is,

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:45.789
and better odds help whether your bets are correlated or not.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.785
Now the case that worries me.

00:04:49.785 --> 00:04:54.041
Abhishaike Mahajan asks whether a drug like PrEP would

00:04:54.041 --> 00:04:57.351
ever come out of a financialized pipeline.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:59.356
Take lenacapavir,

00:04:59.356 --> 00:05:04.713
Gilead's HIV prevention shot that you get twice a year.

00:05:04.713 --> 00:05:06.953
In the PURPOSE 1 trial,

00:05:06.953 --> 00:05:10.361
mostly young women in South Africa,

00:05:10.361 --> 00:05:15.912
2,136 took daily Truvada pills and 39 of them caught HIV.

00:05:16.262 --> 00:05:19.175
2,134 got lenacapavir,

00:05:19.175 --> 00:05:22.353
and not one of them did.

00:05:22.703 --> 00:05:23.599
To show that,

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:27.117
Gilead had to enrol more than five thousand healthy

00:05:27.117 --> 00:05:29.324
people and wait for rare events.

00:05:29.324 --> 00:05:32.910
The US list price is now about twenty-eight thousand

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:33.944
dollars a year.

00:05:33.944 --> 00:05:38.634
Independent modellers think a generic could be made for about forty.

00:05:38.984 --> 00:05:41.854
Put that into Lo's spreadsheet.

00:05:41.854 --> 00:05:44.539
A huge trial, healthy people,

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:47.686
an endpoint that's rare by design,

00:05:47.686 --> 00:05:51.760
and the patients who need it most can't pay.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.815
Nobody can write down those odds,

00:05:54.815 --> 00:05:57.315
so a bond buyer walks away.

00:05:58.617 --> 00:06:00.617
So here's where I've landed.

00:06:00.617 --> 00:06:04.688
Lo was right that drug outcomes are close to independent,

00:06:04.688 --> 00:06:08.188
and even a correlation of point one hurts a bond.

00:06:08.538 --> 00:06:12.059
The money behind a portfolio is far more correlated

00:06:12.059 --> 00:06:13.509
than the drugs in it.

00:06:13.859 --> 00:06:16.910
If you build an AI hub that picks targets

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.026
with one model,

00:06:18.026 --> 00:06:20.408
measure how alike its picks are,

00:06:20.408 --> 00:06:23.013
and cap how many share a mechanism.

00:06:23.013 --> 00:06:26.511
Spend the agents on better odds per drug before

00:06:26.511 --> 00:06:28.670
you spend them on more drugs.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.297
And leave room,

00:06:30.297 --> 00:06:33.533
through philanthropy and public money,

00:06:33.533 --> 00:06:35.066
for the next PrEP.

00:06:35.066 --> 00:06:37.790
It will never fit a spreadsheet.

